Raspberry Pi - Pi-Hole (DNS-Sinkhole) Write-up

I bought my Raspberry Pi 4 from CanaKit on Amazon. It came with:
    Raspberry Pi (obviously) 4GB
    Heat Sinks
    Case and fan
    HDMI cord
    Pre-Imaged 32GB microSD card and USB card reader
    Power Supply

Extras that I had to buy include a USB keyboard and mouse (Wireless works, just needs to be USB) and an ethernet cable.
After I assembled my Pi in its case, it sat in my room, untouched, for about a week because I did not have a keyboard and mouse for it.

Yesterday, May 1, 2026, I took a first trip to Walmart to get the keyboard and mouse.
I don't have a monitor, mainly because I don't have space for it in my tiny apartment bedroom, so I just used my TV.

Once I plugged everything in and booted her up, I went through the process of installing the Raspbian OS to the microSD. This process took probably around 20 minutes.
I opted for the newest version of the OS, which provides a desktop gui and not just a terminal, though you do not really need the desktop unless you try to connect the
Pi to your network via Wi-fi, which I ended up having problems with.

---------[Note: This next section, I had issues, which I will explain]---------

Once the OS was installed, I connected my Pi to my internet via WI-FI. In order to set up the Pi for a Pi-hole, I had to set a static IP address for the Pi to act as my DNS. 
For this project, I set mine to 192.168.0.106. To do this, I went to my router's web interface, found the DHCP section, went to Reservations, and bound the Pi's MAC to the IP.
Then, reboot the Pi.

After rebooting, I ran ifconfig in the terminal to make sure that the Pi had reconnected with that IP.
The next thing I did was run the following command to install Pi-Hole onto my Pi: curl -sSL https://install.pi-hole.net | bash
This begins the process of installing and after maybe 30 seconds, it should bring up a gui for the installer. 
There will be a couple messages about the installer making changes and ensure that your Pi has been set to a static IP. 
Once that was done, I set up the Upstream DNS Provider as Cloudflare (they all have their own attributes, it does not matter too much for this). 
On the next page, I did install using the third-party list. I enabled query logging and selected "show everything".

After that, the installer will give you a confirmation screen with your ADMIN password to access your Pi-Hole dashboard.
Take a picture of it. Optionally, you can change your password by running the following command in the terminal: sudo pihole setpassword
Make sure you save your password somewhere secure or else you will not be able to access the dashboard

Up until this point, everything I had done was directly on the Pi, however there were a few tutorials that did the installation by
SSHing into the Pi. Out of curiosity, I tried, and found that my host and Pi could not establish an connection, despite them being
connected to the same router. This is because the Kit comes with a pre-imaged microSD card, and by default, SSH is disabled. So to fix,
I had to click the Raspberry in the top left corner of my Pi desktop >> Preferences >> Raspberry Pi Configuration >> Interfaces.

I tried again to SSH into the Pi, but still to no avail. Next, I decided to test connectivity in both directions using ping. Neither device 
to ping the other, though they were both able to talk to the router. I checked Windows Firewall on my computer to see if any outbound rules 
were preventing the pings from going through, and nothing caught my eye (I should have also checked the inbound rules at that point but I did not). 
To double check, I connected both my computer and my Pi to my phone's hotspot, and they were able to communicate. So, it seemed that there was 
something isolating the two devices, I'm not sure why though. To triple check, I took another trip to Walmart to get an ethernet cable to directly 
connect the Pi to my router through a LAN port.

At some point, I think my Pi updated, and I believe this reset its IP configuration because it rejoined my network with the IP 192.168.0.102, 
which is not what I had it as. So, I tried to go back to the router web interface and reassign the MAC to the new IP address, but the Pi would 
not take the static. So, I manually changed it in the etc/dhcpcd.conf file by adding an eth0 section at the end of it. It looked a little bit like:

interface eth0
static ip_address=192.168.0.106/24
static routers=192.168.0.1
static domain_name_servers=192.168.0.1 8.8.8.8

------[Problem resolved]------

After that, my Pi was showing the static IP in ifconfig. I was then able to ping the Pi from my host, but when I tried to ping my host from the Pi, 
traffic was being blocked. So I went back to the Windows Firewall and checked inbound rules, and while nothing screamed "blocking ICMP), I was suspicious.
So, I made a new rule to accept ICMPv4 just to make sure that it was not a connectivity problem and rather, just my Firewall. And that solved it.

Now that my connection was working both ways, I was able to get SSH to work. Not that it mattered, but just to stay consistent with the tutorial I had seen,
I went through the whole process again remotely. Now, to get to the dashboard, I went to {Pi-IP-ADDRESS}/admin/login. You can also put https:// infront of it
for a potentially more secure connection.

At this point, there is likely to be no activity on the dashboard, and this is because the Pi is not yet set up as the DNS, so any ads or trackers are still
being resolved by whatever the DNS is in the router's web interface. If you prefer, you can just change one device so that it does not affect your entire network.
I tried out both. On my windows machine, I went to Network and Internet Settings >> Advanced network settings and selected my wi-fi adapter. Then I clicked View
Additional Properties >> Edit IP Assignment and change it to manual and enabled IPv4. I assigned the computer to the same IP that it was before with the same subnet
mask as well. Then, I put the router's IP in the gateway field. In Preferred DNS, I put the IP of the Raspberry Pi.

It may be worth putting a backup DNS just in case the Pi-Hole breaks, otherwise your device loses internet connectivity. This would be especially important if you
set your router's DNS to the Pi-Hole, because then your entire network would fail without a backup. To make my Pi-hole my DNS for the entire network, i just changed
the DNS field in the DHCP section of my web interface.

Once your DNS is changed to the Pi-Hole, you should see some activity in the Pi-hole. If you go to Query Log, you should see all kinds of random queries, and you can
even test it by going to google.com and watching the query pop up in the log. You can also test the ad blocking aspect by going to sites like CNN.com or FoxNews.com,
or any ad-heavy site and seeing if those ad boxes are empty. 

Some other observations:

Queries are not recorded if you are using a VPN (as expected)
Queries are seen even if using private browsing
Play a mobile game on your phone and watch ad queries flowing into the query log

Lessons Learned:

Troubleshooting
IP Assignment/DHCP/DNS
Firewall Rules
Logging and Monitoring
